program is I did, I think, an independent
study with an education professor,

00:15:17.439 --> 00:15:21.270
and that's where I did my
business plan to start my school.

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:24.190
So It was in one of those classes.

00:15:24.190 --> 00:15:27.170
We had an education class,
we had a guest speaker.

00:15:27.170 --> 00:15:31.990
And we did a case study on a woman who
started a middle school in Mountain View.

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:36.110
And I talked to her and she said,
I said, I want to start my own school.

00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:37.210
This sounds so cool.

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:40.656
And she said, we'll do a business plan,
like work it out and

00:15:40.656 --> 00:15:44.871
going through that process will really
help you and then you can just start one.

00:15:44.871 --> 00:15:47.020
I said okay, so that's what I did.

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:50.584
And then because the GSB and
the ED school are so flexible,

00:15:50.584 --> 00:15:55.471
you do have that opportunity to sort
of create the curriculum you want and

00:15:55.471 --> 00:16:00.598
Looking back at my business plan I made,
I had a timeline in it.

00:16:00.598 --> 00:16:05.326
And I think in my timeline, I said,
I would open doors September

00:16:05.326 --> 00:16:10.420
2012 of my school, and
I ended up starting it October 2012.

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:15.040
So it was sort of cool that I was
able to bring this plan to life.

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:19.803
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks, Claire, do you want to
share just how you decided to do

00:16:19.803 --> 00:16:23.537
the joint degree and
how did it help you in your career?

00:16:23.537 --> 00:16:29.430
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I was pretty involved in
education and undergrad at Stanford.

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:35.269
So I felt like I had some
exposure to professors already.

00:16:35.269 --> 00:16:39.741
But I came in sort of feeling, so
you can apply at the outset or

00:16:39.741 --> 00:16:45.560
you can start your MBA and then apply,
and I had applied at the very beginning.

00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:51.705
I think, first and foremost,
it was just a really intentional decision

00:16:51.705 --> 00:16:54.830
that I wanted to pursue
education as a career.

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:58.777
And the GSB can pull you in
a lot of different directions.

00:16:58.777 --> 00:17:03.220
And I felt like the commitment to
the ED school and to the group that was

00:17:03.220 --> 00:17:07.291
in the joint degree program was
a really helpful anchor for me.

00:17:07.291 --> 00:17:10.971
I felt like I made really good friends,

00:17:10.971 --> 00:17:15.333
who we still continue to
meet in a small group.

00:17:15.333 --> 00:17:19.933
Some are in investing,
some are working in product and

00:17:19.933 --> 00:17:23.542
just kind of all over
the map in education.

00:17:23.542 --> 00:17:27.871
But a really helpful set of kind
of professional friendships,

00:17:27.871 --> 00:17:30.657
I would say, that continue to serve me.

00:17:30.657 --> 00:17:35.580
And it was just a helpful informal space
when I was there too to be able to kind

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:40.505
of, like Anuja's talking about,
kind of lean on different professors,

00:17:40.505 --> 00:17:41.771
lean on students.

00:17:41.771 --> 00:17:46.896
And be able to explore different things
with the sort of added lens of education.

00:17:46.896 --> 00:17:50.989
And I had previously studied education
with more of a teaching lens, and

00:17:50.989 --> 00:17:55.302
this felt more of an organizational
lens with a couple different pathways.

00:17:55.302 --> 00:18:00.394
Which was just sort of coming into
the business school with this idea that

00:18:00.394 --> 00:18:05.470
I maybe wanted to think about building
a business and managing people.

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:09.200
It was just a helpful layer that
was more kind of industry specific.

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:12.869
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks for sharing that.

00:18:12.869 --> 00:18:16.535
Christina, I want to gear the next
question towards you to start

00:18:16.535 --> 00:18:17.580
with answering.

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:20.407
And again, any of the panelists,
feel free to chime in.

00:18:20.407 --> 00:18:24.473
But we have a question around just
how does the GSB support you if

00:18:24.473 --> 00:18:27.478
you are starting something
in social impact?

00:18:27.478 --> 00:18:31.784
So if you have an idea,
but maybe not a budget.

00:18:31.784 --> 00:18:37.947
And you mentioned already the fellowship
that you received and were part of.

00:18:37.947 --> 00:18:40.503
So if you could share just about that.

00:18:40.503 --> 00:18:44.379
But also in any other ways that the GSB
may have helped you on your journey

00:18:44.379 --> 00:18:47.882
towards starting something,
specifically in social impact.

00:18:49.763 --> 00:18:51.305
&gt;&gt; Yeah, definitely, so

00:18:51.305 --> 00:18:56.430
just to share a little bit more about
the social innovation fellowship.

00:18:56.430 --> 00:19:00.830
So each year, there are two people
that are awarded the fellowship.

00:19:02.170 --> 00:19:07.300
And as of last year, it was only for
people launching nonprofits.

00:19:07.300 --> 00:19:13.155
But now it's for people who are just
launching social ventures in general.

00:19:13.155 --> 00:19:16.928
So they could be for-profit or nonprofit.

00:19:16.928 --> 00:19:17.908
Can everyone hear me?

00:19:17.908 --> 00:19:18.661
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:19:18.661 --> 00:19:20.242
&gt;&gt; I just thought that it
might have frozen, okay.

00:19:20.242 --> 00:19:21.899
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; For a second,

00:19:21.899 --> 00:19:24.770
I thought it froze because
the three faces I saw really.

00:19:24.770 --> 00:19:28.102
So they were listening intently to me,
I guess.

00:19:28.102 --> 00:19:33.970
So I won't go too much
into that right now.

00:19:33.970 --> 00:19:36.706
I know that the link was
sent out in the chat and

00:19:36.706 --> 00:19:39.169
because it is only two people each year.

00:19:39.169 --> 00:19:43.420
I think it might serve me better to share
more about the other options as well.

00:19:43.420 --> 00:19:46.212
So for that fellowship, however,

00:19:46.212 --> 00:19:52.030
they do provide you $110,000 and
a year of advising to launch it.

00:19:52.030 --> 00:19:54.630
So it's a really wonderful opportunity.

00:19:54.630 --> 00:19:58.373
But some of the other opportunities that
could take place is one over the summer.

00:19:58.373 --> 00:20:03.136
So if you want to test out some of your
ideas to launch your own social impact

00:20:03.136 --> 00:20:06.900
adventure or if you want to
join one that already exists.

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:11.207
There's a funding that the CMC,
the Career Management Center,

00:20:11.207 --> 00:20:16.768
as well as the center that's over social
innovation entrepreneurship provides for

00:20:16.768 --> 00:20:21.718
you to not be concerned about the salary
that you're paid over the summer.

00:20:21.718 --> 00:20:26.848
So they will essentially double your
salary so that it's on par with

00:20:26.848 --> 00:20:32.796
what your other classmates would be
making in a more traditional internship.

00:20:32.796 --> 00:20:37.694
So that's really just unique, and
I think it shows how intentional the GSB

00:20:37.694 --> 00:20:42.606
is about trying to remove any financial
barriers to go into social impact.

00:20:42.606 --> 00:20:45.356
Another example beyond the internships and

00:20:45.356 --> 00:20:48.931
beyond the fellowship is
the loan forgiveness program.

00:20:48.931 --> 00:20:55.190
So if you decide to enter
into a social impact career.

00:20:55.190 --> 00:20:59.039
And if you are making below a certain
cutoff, and I won't quote any specific

00:20:59.039 --> 00:21:02.780
numbers because it might have changed,
but your loans could be forgiven.

00:21:02.780 --> 00:21:07.472
So that's just a third example
of how the GSB really does take

00:21:07.472 --> 00:21:12.911
the budget part of it that was
mentioned in the question into account.

00:21:12.911 --> 00:21:17.209
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks for sharing,
that's really helpful I think for

00:21:17.209 --> 00:21:19.285
those on the call to hear that.

00:21:19.285 --> 00:21:22.049
And just to chime in a little
bit about loan forgiveness,

00:21:22.049 --> 00:21:25.689
I also am not going to say an exact number
just because that always can change.

00:21:25.689 --> 00:21:28.039
But I encourage you if you want
to learn about that program,

00:21:28.039 --> 00:21:29.700
it is something that is offered.

00:21:29.700 --> 00:21:33.020
So were you to come to the GSB and
take on loans and

00:21:33.020 --> 00:21:36.595
then go work in a nonprofit or
in the public sector.

00:21:36.595 --> 00:21:41.737
You could be eligible then for this loan
forgiveness program, which again is one

00:21:41.737 --> 00:21:47.690
of the ways that the GSB is committed to
helping those pursue careers in this area.

00:21:47.690 --> 00:21:53.295
So I do want to jump, there was
a question and they're interchanging.

00:21:53.295 --> 00:21:56.393
But there was one
specifically around clubs or

00:21:56.393 --> 00:22:00.287
extracurriculars that may have
helped kind of shape you or

00:22:00.287 --> 00:22:04.430
exposed you to certain ideas or
people or things throughout.

00:22:04.430 --> 00:22:08.564
So if you could speak to, and
we'll start with Daniel, what clubs or

00:22:08.564 --> 00:22:13.057
extracurriculars you may have been
involved in that also kind of helped you

00:22:13.057 --> 00:22:16.078
on your path towards social impact or
education?

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:22.832
&gt;&gt; [COUGH] I think, as I mentioned,
I think our rubble fellows, impact fund,

00:22:22.832 --> 00:22:27.835
and joint degree, those three things,
it's a big commitment already.

00:22:27.835 --> 00:22:33.038
Then the other ones, Education Club
is a nice social thing to basically

00:22:33.038 --> 00:22:37.991
meet people from the GSB interchange
education from the ED school.

00:22:37.991 --> 00:22:44.862
That are also I think they can join, and
basically a newsletter of things related,

00:22:44.862 --> 00:22:49.487
job opportunities and
just information on education.

00:22:49.487 --> 00:22:55.567
Then everything related to
the Social Innovation Center,

00:22:55.567 --> 00:22:59.709
especially that having good relation.

00:22:59.709 --> 00:23:04.492
Yeah, like an advisor that you can,
like for me was Naomi Beyer, for example.

00:23:04.492 --> 00:23:09.960
And just having regular catches with her,
just to her the things I was doing.

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:13.769
And she always try to connect me
with people that were relevant, so

00:23:13.769 --> 00:23:16.433
I mean stuff that was
relevant to my interest.

00:23:16.433 --> 00:23:20.325
At the end, yeah,
I think joining a club is good,

00:23:20.325 --> 00:23:25.077
because you'll see who are people
interested in the theme.

00:23:25.077 --> 00:23:30.482
But at the end, literally, you can
do many things outside of a club and

00:23:30.482 --> 00:23:34.067
just literally doing independent projects.

00:23:34.067 --> 00:23:36.870
Or working outside part time,
or doing an internship, or

00:23:36.870 --> 00:23:40.430
doing a special part with any
institution you're interested.

00:23:40.430 --> 00:23:44.436
For example, with the impact fund,
the advisor of the impact fund is

00:23:44.436 --> 00:23:47.560
Hall Ventures,
which is the largest set of VC funds.

00:23:47.560 --> 00:23:52.278
They advise education
the under mostly GSB years.

00:23:52.278 --> 00:23:55.819
And just getting to know them
from the impact fund, I'd say, or

00:23:55.819 --> 00:23:59.150
however you guys to help you open,
invest in Latin America.

00:23:59.150 --> 00:24:02.849
That was one of the most
vocal experiences for me.

00:24:02.849 --> 00:24:07.027
And I got credit for that and I just got
an incredible relationship with them and

00:24:07.027 --> 00:24:08.246
learn a lot from them.

00:24:08.246 --> 00:24:14.397
So I think just knowing that
while you were a student and

00:24:14.397 --> 00:24:18.554
under GSB credential are just huge.

00:24:18.554 --> 00:24:22.151
To get the amount of returns
that come to you and

00:24:22.151 --> 00:24:25.576
you can do on the side
are literally unique.

00:24:25.576 --> 00:24:29.977
So I would highly encourage everyone
to be creative and think, okay,

00:24:29.977 --> 00:24:31.550
what am I interested in?

00:24:31.550 --> 00:24:33.745
What institutions are the people
want to reach out?

00:24:33.745 --> 00:24:37.606
And why am I unique in my theme?

00:24:37.606 --> 00:24:40.235
And just tell a good story to get and

00:24:40.235 --> 00:24:45.238
create the opportunity that you
want based under your interest.

00:24:45.238 --> 00:24:49.499
&gt;&gt; Hey, thanks, Claire, could you
chime in and share maybe a club or

00:24:49.499 --> 00:24:54.612
extracurricular that influenced you or
helped you during your time at the GSB.

00:24:54.612 --> 00:24:59.574
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I actually didn't
formally joined a lot of clubs.

00:24:59.574 --> 00:25:04.987
I sort of didn't want to be spread
too thin and so I focused a lot

00:25:04.987 --> 00:25:11.163
on taking a lot of classes across
the GSB and the school of education.

00:25:11.163 --> 00:25:14.849
And I went to a lot of office hours,
sort of got a good read,

00:25:14.849 --> 00:25:18.252
these are sort of informal
things to get involved in.

00:25:18.252 --> 00:25:22.572
But got a good read quickly on kind of
which professors were really invested in

00:25:22.572 --> 00:25:23.578
student growth.

00:25:23.578 --> 00:25:28.832
And then I did a few different independent
studies and internships as kind

00:25:28.832 --> 00:25:33.851
of mini tests to answer a couple
questions for myself about my career.

00:25:33.851 --> 00:25:36.378
So, did I want to start my own school?

00:25:36.378 --> 00:25:38.894
Did I want to start a school
from within the network?

00:25:38.894 --> 00:25:40.302
What were different models?

00:25:40.302 --> 00:25:45.331
How could I do projects with those types
of institutions to really get to know

00:25:45.331 --> 00:25:50.775
them with professors that would help me
kind of pull apart some different themes.

00:25:50.775 --> 00:25:55.750
And then I spent a lot
of time getting to know

00:25:55.750 --> 00:26:00.865
my classmates and
trying to carve out time to

00:26:00.865 --> 00:26:05.998
sort of learn more about their background.

00:26:05.998 --> 00:26:09.665
I sort of only knew teaching and
working in schools and

00:26:09.665 --> 00:26:13.100
learned a ton from other
people's experiences.

00:26:13.100 --> 00:26:18.441
In terms of how to build or not build
culture across different industries,

00:26:18.441 --> 00:26:22.780
just sort of things like that
from informal conversations.

00:26:22.780 --> 00:26:27.988
And I found the summer internship
program through Smith that Christina,

00:26:27.988 --> 00:26:30.172
I think, referenced really,

00:26:30.172 --> 00:26:35.056
really useful because that was
a subsidized nonprofit internship.

00:26:35.056 --> 00:26:39.199
I worked for a venture philanthropy
fund that supported schools, so

00:26:39.199 --> 00:26:43.201
it was a better chance for
me to kind of understand 30,000 but

00:26:43.201 --> 00:26:46.147
few did that feel engaging and
meaningful to me.

00:26:46.147 --> 00:26:50.742
And was part of a kind of network of
classmates who had also participated

00:26:50.742 --> 00:26:51.900
in that program.

00:26:51.900 --> 00:26:55.678
So that felt like a good way to sort
of pressure test some things and

00:26:55.678 --> 00:26:58.164
have some folks to be
able to reflect with.

00:26:58.164 --> 00:27:01.564
But I think sort of throughout
it was either formal or

00:27:01.564 --> 00:27:05.212
informal ways to just have
conversations with people.

00:27:05.212 --> 00:27:10.280
And use the opportunity to be
more self-reflective that I

00:27:10.280 --> 00:27:16.809
didn't feel like I had when I was working
and more kind of focused on my job.

00:27:16.809 --> 00:27:18.266
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks.

00:27:18.266 --> 00:27:23.559
Christina, could you share if there was,
I guess, an experience whether

00:27:23.559 --> 00:27:28.697
it's extracurricular club related
that also helped you along the way?

00:27:28.697 --> 00:27:32.599
If it's been already mentioned
you can say that, [LAUGH] too.

00:27:32.599 --> 00:27:38.309
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean, I think honestly I
would say I am like clear where I was.

00:27:38.309 --> 00:27:41.495
I don't know if it came from being
burned out as a teacher in the past.

00:27:41.495 --> 00:27:46.462
But I just really wanted to conserve
my energy and be intentional, so

00:27:46.462 --> 00:27:48.871
I didn't really join any clubs.

00:27:48.871 --> 00:27:53.300
The one extra club in which I was
really involved was the BBSA, so

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:56.267
the black business student association.

00:27:56.267 --> 00:27:59.928
So there's tons of different
affinity groups and

00:27:59.928 --> 00:28:05.519
I was able to help lead the revival of
the annual conference when I was there.

00:28:05.519 --> 00:28:10.482
So I think that was a really great
experience in just leadership,

00:28:10.482 --> 00:28:14.002
public speaking,
dealing with logistics and

00:28:14.002 --> 00:28:18.980
operations which has served me
well now running my own business.

00:28:18.980 --> 00:28:22.402
But I would say the one other
thing I will make a note of,

00:28:22.402 --> 00:28:26.139
it was a class but
it's called social ventures practicum.

00:28:26.139 --> 00:28:30.871
So it's kind of like startup garage,
which you all may have heard of, but

00:28:30.871 --> 00:28:34.139
it's focused on social
ventures in particular.

00:28:34.139 --> 00:28:38.925
And I actually was able to recruit six
different students from all across

00:28:38.925 --> 00:28:41.124
the Stanford campus undergrad,

00:28:41.124 --> 00:28:44.749
different grad programs to
help work on idea for hope.

00:28:44.749 --> 00:28:48.555
And that was my first time really
being able to manage people.

00:28:48.555 --> 00:28:52.107
It was quite an interesting experience,

00:28:52.107 --> 00:28:57.392
learned a lot of things so
that I could be a lot better at it now.

00:28:57.392 --> 00:28:59.468
But they just want to note that, but

00:28:59.468 --> 00:29:03.227
I would say no other specific
clubs as related to social impact.

00:29:03.227 --> 00:29:08.632
&gt;&gt; Christina, I have a question, was the
social venture practicum one quarter or

00:29:08.632 --> 00:29:10.984
was that over multiple quarters?

00:29:10.984 --> 00:29:17.902
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there's over one quarter and
it was taught by two professors one

00:29:17.902 --> 00:29:23.341
Bill Yuan who was more of
like a consulting background.

00:29:23.341 --> 00:29:27.732
And another one Kim Starkey now and

00:29:27.732 --> 00:29:32.741
she's focused more on philanthropy.

00:29:32.741 --> 00:29:36.533
So it was a really interesting
combination of the two.

00:29:36.533 --> 00:29:38.263
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks for sharing.

00:29:38.263 --> 00:29:42.974
We have a question and that's been kind
of up voted to know what is it that

00:29:42.974 --> 00:29:46.996
caught you most off guard when
you started business school?

00:29:46.996 --> 00:29:49.898
So what has surprised
you most about the GSB?

00:29:49.898 --> 00:29:54.767
And you can feel free to answer that, I'll
probably start with Daniel since you're

00:29:54.767 --> 00:29:57.177
the most recent graduate of the program.

00:29:57.177 --> 00:30:02.743
But I also want to for many of our
panelists, for many of you it was a change

00:30:02.743 --> 00:30:08.156
in terms of coming to business school
from what you were doing before.

00:30:08.156 --> 00:30:11.588
And there is another question, so
feel free to kind of what surprised you?

00:30:11.588 --> 00:30:16.857
But also in your first year,
maybe share what that adjustment was like.

00:30:16.857 --> 00:30:21.896
So in addition to your surprise what
helped you adjust to being in business

00:30:21.896 --> 00:30:26.551
school especially if you were
pivoting from a different industry?

00:30:26.551 --> 00:30:29.143
So again, we'll start with Daniel.

00:30:29.143 --> 00:30:36.893
&gt;&gt; Sure, [COUGH] so I think that the
intensity of the first couple quarters,

00:30:36.893 --> 00:30:41.989
everything, it's a lot
of things basically.

00:30:41.989 --> 00:30:47.306
And there's a lot of incredible
unique opportunity you think

00:30:47.306 --> 00:30:54.066
that every coffee chat, every BBL events
in the afternoons, every speaker.

00:30:54.066 --> 00:30:58.089
They're all like probably one of the most
unique experience I've ever had to meet

00:30:58.089 --> 00:30:59.972
this person or hear this person talk.

00:30:59.972 --> 00:31:06.708
So if any of you are intellectually
curious and like different things it'll,

00:31:06.708 --> 00:31:11.925
[LAUGH] be a muscle of practicing
when you really understand.

00:31:11.925 --> 00:31:15.169
Okay, what am I here for and
what am I going to spend my limited time?

00:31:15.169 --> 00:31:20.417
And that involves personal stuff,
friends, classes,

00:31:20.417 --> 00:31:27.101
BBLs, and working on independent
products so that's the first thing.

00:31:27.101 --> 00:31:29.817
Then the first year classes are very,
well,

00:31:29.817 --> 00:31:34.403
it depends on the level on the other and
experience each version comes with.

00:31:34.403 --> 00:31:40.835
But they could be the more traditional,
the one that the way you think and

00:31:40.835 --> 00:31:47.184
have the kind of a core curriculum and
things that you got to go through.

00:31:47.184 --> 00:31:52.816
For me I started kind of working part
time since second quarter probably.

00:31:52.816 --> 00:31:55.899
And basically it was really good that at

00:31:55.899 --> 00:32:00.393
the ending the grades of
the GSB don't matter that much.

00:32:00.393 --> 00:32:03.838
If you want to do great, you can go and
try to be an Arbuckle fellow, sorry,

00:32:03.838 --> 00:32:05.770
an Archie Miller which is like top 10%.

00:32:05.770 --> 00:32:08.969
But if you just want to
pass you can only pass.

00:32:08.969 --> 00:32:13.678
I think that's one of the misconception
is that it's very hard.

00:32:13.678 --> 00:32:16.999
The classes are hard, and you have to
get good grades and you got to study and

00:32:16.999 --> 00:32:17.914
do a bunch of stuff.

00:32:17.914 --> 00:32:21.649
But if you just want to pass and
focus on certain classes.

00:32:21.649 --> 00:32:25.825
Or in others maybe don't focus much and
just do the bare minimum because you

00:32:25.825 --> 00:32:28.223
want to put your time
to non academic stuff.

00:32:28.223 --> 00:32:32.816
You can do it and that gives you a lot
of freedom and I think that makes

00:32:32.816 --> 00:32:37.837
the community be much stronger and
the people want it to help each other.

00:32:37.837 --> 00:32:42.678
I never felt any type of
competitiveness in terms of,

00:32:42.678 --> 00:32:47.736
my classmates in the class,
just helping each other and

00:32:47.736 --> 00:32:50.657
I think that's great and that.

00:32:50.657 --> 00:32:54.546
After the first quarter because first,
you got to get your first classes and

00:32:54.546 --> 00:32:56.991
make sure that you pass
on your neck operation.

00:32:56.991 --> 00:32:57.983
After the first quarter.

00:32:57.983 --> 00:33:02.132
I never felt kind of a lot of
stress in terms of academics.

00:33:02.132 --> 00:33:05.763
I think that's a huge thing for.

00:33:05.763 --> 00:33:07.111
&gt;&gt; Hey, thanks for sharing.

00:33:07.111 --> 00:33:11.447
And I'd love for each of the panellists to
answer this just what has surprised you

00:33:11.447 --> 00:33:14.849
most and then maybe any tips or
learnings from your first year.

00:33:14.849 --> 00:33:19.062
So in Asia, why don't we go
around at least how I see you

00:33:19.062 --> 00:33:23.682
guys would echo [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah a lot of what Daniel said, but for

00:33:23.682 --> 00:33:27.684
me, I do remember thinking
it was quite rigorous.

00:33:27.684 --> 00:33:30.080
It was much busier than I was expecting,

00:33:30.080 --> 00:33:34.944
because I don't know if people still say
it, but people would say, Business School,

00:33:34.944 --> 00:33:38.071
is sort of a vacation, or
it's fun, or something.

00:33:38.071 --> 00:33:41.084
A lot of people come when
you're coming from finance, and

00:33:41.084 --> 00:33:45.160
working these 80 hour weeks, I thought
Cool, I'm burnt out I need a break.

00:33:45.160 --> 00:33:47.812
But it was still incredibly busy.

00:33:47.812 --> 00:33:51.384
But in a much different way in
a much more engaging way, a fun way.

00:33:51.384 --> 00:33:55.924
And in there's classes and there's parties
and clubs and just have everything

00:33:55.924 --> 00:34:00.342
travel experiences and my classmates
they're like, hey, it's Tuesday.

00:34:00.342 --> 00:34:01.696
Let's go to Vegas for the night.

00:34:01.696 --> 00:34:02.840
Come back on Wednesday.

00:34:02.840 --> 00:34:04.668
I don't know if it's still the same.

00:34:04.668 --> 00:34:09.007
But balancing all of that and
getting over this FOMO right?

00:34:09.007 --> 00:34:13.946
The fear of missing out and learning how
to really focus your efforts on the people

00:34:13.946 --> 00:34:18.900
you want to spend time with in the classes
you want to spend time with and the trips.

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:23.670
Just because everyone's going
to the World Cup doesn't

00:34:23.670 --> 00:34:28.649
mean you have to go and so
I think that was not overwhelming.

00:34:28.649 --> 00:34:30.809
But maybe and then once you figure it out,

00:34:30.809 --> 00:34:33.783
you have your core group you
learn more about who you are.

00:34:33.783 --> 00:34:37.142
And then I think those
are the friendships and

00:34:37.142 --> 00:34:41.309
relationships you take with
you when you leave the GSB.

00:34:41.309 --> 00:34:42.508
&gt;&gt; Christina, could you share?

00:34:45.623 --> 00:34:50.295
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I feel like I could just
say ditto to all that, honestly.

00:34:50.295 --> 00:34:55.587
Yeah, I think for me, I was really
taken aback by just how busy it was.

00:34:55.587 --> 00:35:02.011
I remember it was the first time that
I was really glued to my calendar.

00:35:02.011 --> 00:35:06.701
And it's like, kind of funny thinking back
to it, but like Daniel was talking about

00:35:06.701 --> 00:35:11.194
the coffee chats and all that, but even
the social things are on your calendar.

00:35:11.194 --> 00:35:14.349
Like everyone just sends
a calendar invite for everything.

00:35:14.349 --> 00:35:17.166
And I wasn't used to that before.

00:35:17.166 --> 00:35:19.962
So it was the kind of thing
where it was like, all right.

00:35:19.962 --> 00:35:21.575
It's Monday at 10am.

00:35:21.575 --> 00:35:26.090
Let me check my calendar to remind
myself what am I doing at 1pm today.

00:35:26.090 --> 00:35:31.390
So it was very busy but
in a good way, very exciting,

00:35:31.390 --> 00:35:36.090
so many different unique opportunities,
but

00:35:36.090 --> 00:35:40.199
it kind of felt like Constant activity.

00:35:40.199 --> 00:35:43.079
So I think the first year I kind of
did let myself like Claire mentioned,

00:35:43.079 --> 00:35:44.961
you get pulled a lot of
different directions.

00:35:44.961 --> 00:35:47.283
I let myself get pulled a lot
of different directions.

00:35:47.283 --> 00:35:50.800
That second year,
I was able to be more intentional and

00:35:50.800 --> 00:35:53.252
figure out what I wanted to say yes to.

00:35:53.252 --> 00:35:55.718
So that's what I would say kind of,

00:35:55.718 --> 00:35:59.267
echoing the same theme
that's been shared so far.

00:35:59.267 --> 00:36:03.985
&gt;&gt; Great and Claire, could you also chime
in if there's anything else to add in

00:36:03.985 --> 00:36:08.718
terms of what surprised you and any tips
in your first year that you learned?

00:36:08.718 --> 00:36:10.513
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:36:10.513 --> 00:36:11.721
Probably two big things.

00:36:11.721 --> 00:36:16.477
One I had a really hard time with
the fact that it felt like I had

00:36:16.477 --> 00:36:20.303
gone from this place where
everything I did was so

00:36:20.303 --> 00:36:26.200
other people could be successful to
being like only worried about myself.

00:36:26.200 --> 00:36:31.164
And Grad school is just like very self
indulgent and that's wonderful and

00:36:31.164 --> 00:36:35.903
I think I just took me a little while
to name that and feel okay about it.

00:36:35.903 --> 00:36:41.216
And I think once I sort of was like this
is just a different time in your life and

00:36:41.216 --> 00:36:46.464
you're here to learn so that you can
be more useful to people in the future.

00:36:46.464 --> 00:36:50.140
That was a sort of helpful
mindset shift for me.

00:36:50.140 --> 00:36:53.903
But I think because of what these folks
have said around just being so busy.

00:36:53.903 --> 00:36:58.436
It's easy to sort of be distracted and
not not give yourself enough time to pay

00:36:58.436 --> 00:37:02.219
attention to kind of how you're
feeling about the transition.

00:37:02.219 --> 00:37:07.555
And then the second thing is that I
found the core classes really hard.

00:37:07.555 --> 00:37:11.290
And I was acutely aware of what good
teaching looked like and I was sort of

00:37:11.290 --> 00:37:14.973
like lectures are really hard for
me to learn in and I don't know that.

00:37:14.973 --> 00:37:19.295
I'm absorbing all the sort of financial
literacy that I came here to learn.

00:37:19.295 --> 00:37:22.608
And a lot of folks in the class
know a lot more than I do.

00:37:22.608 --> 00:37:25.224
And so I felt a little bit insecure
actually in the beginning.

00:37:25.224 --> 00:37:29.765
And, I think,
over time started to feel like okay,

00:37:29.765 --> 00:37:32.977
this is part of a larger experience.

00:37:32.977 --> 00:37:34.181
I'm learning these things.

00:37:34.181 --> 00:37:35.006
They're new to me.

00:37:35.006 --> 00:37:36.037
That's okay.

00:37:36.037 --> 00:37:40.370
And, also some of the other
people management things and

00:37:40.370 --> 00:37:45.243
EQ things that have more exposure to and
managing other adults,

00:37:45.243 --> 00:37:50.042
classrooms or things that are going to
be relevant at some point.

00:37:50.042 --> 00:37:55.552
And so
I think it was just sort of being okay

00:37:55.552 --> 00:38:01.070
with being challenged and feeling like.

00:38:01.070 --> 00:38:06.011
I think if I were to give some
advice around that it's just sort of

00:38:06.011 --> 00:38:10.052
like trusting the process and
sort of feeling like.

00:38:10.052 --> 00:38:13.750
Even if you don't feel really
confident in the moment that your

00:38:13.750 --> 00:38:18.006
strengths will play out in different
ways across the two years or three or

00:38:18.006 --> 00:38:20.686
however long you spend
earning your degree.

00:38:20.686 --> 00:38:22.738
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks for sharing.

00:38:22.738 --> 00:38:25.834
We have a question around
global experience.

00:38:25.834 --> 00:38:31.577
And specifically whether any of you did
pursue or fulfill your global experience

00:38:31.577 --> 00:38:37.164
requirement with a focus on social
impact or education, which I don't know.

00:38:37.164 --> 00:38:40.277
But I'd love for
some of you to share about that.

00:38:40.277 --> 00:38:41.364
So, Christina,

00:38:41.364 --> 00:38:46.080
could you share what you did to fulfill
your global experience requirement?

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:48.325
And actually before you answer, just for

00:38:48.325 --> 00:38:51.030
everyone on the call because
this was a question.

00:38:51.030 --> 00:38:54.087
Some of you might be wondering what
is the global experience requirement,

00:38:54.087 --> 00:38:55.550
which you can Google and find more.

00:38:55.550 --> 00:38:59.074
Out more, you can find about
more information online.

00:38:59.074 --> 00:39:02.752
But briefly, there's a requirement for
those who come to

00:39:02.752 --> 00:39:07.644
the GSB to spend time in a country they've
never lived in or worked in before.

00:39:07.644 --> 00:39:12.341
And this can be fulfilled in many
different ways from a global study trip to

00:39:12.341 --> 00:39:16.535
another acronym called a G Max,
which is a one month internship.

00:39:16.535 --> 00:39:19.223
So I'm going to just throw that
out there just to clarify for

00:39:19.223 --> 00:39:21.750
those on the call who maybe
didn't know what that is.

00:39:21.750 --> 00:39:26.104
And so Christina, if you could share what
how did you fulfill your global experience

00:39:26.104 --> 00:39:27.832
for permanent that'd be great.

00:39:27.832 --> 00:39:32.657
&gt;&gt; Sure, so
I went to Greece which was amazing.

00:39:32.657 --> 00:39:35.952
When during the springtime,
and I think Greece and

00:39:35.952 --> 00:39:40.547
who knows there might be someone on
here from there, but at least to me,

00:39:40.547 --> 00:39:44.932
I just thought of it as okay,
this beautiful tourist destination.

00:39:44.932 --> 00:39:49.900
But what we focus on what we learned
about was how the debt crisis

00:39:49.900 --> 00:39:54.789
has Impacted by the inequity in
health care for Greek people.

00:39:54.789 --> 00:39:57.147
So it was extremely eye opening and

00:39:57.147 --> 00:40:01.996
I would say it was related to I ultimately
decided That accessibility when

00:40:01.996 --> 00:40:05.458
it comes to mental health was really
important to me, and that's something

00:40:05.458 --> 00:40:08.555
I didn't see in the students that I
work with every day as a teacher.

00:40:08.555 --> 00:40:13.432
So I think it just opened my eyes to
basically, how when there is a debt

00:40:13.432 --> 00:40:18.478
crisis, how tourism tries to kind of
offset some of those impacts, but

00:40:18.478 --> 00:40:23.804
who really ends up getting impacted
the most are the people who live there.

00:40:23.804 --> 00:40:27.921
And that Healthcare as challenging
as it can be in the United States

00:40:27.921 --> 00:40:31.610
is definitely not something to
be taken for granted at all.

00:40:31.610 --> 00:40:35.089
So yeah,
it was a very interesting experience.

00:40:35.089 --> 00:40:38.869
&gt;&gt; Great, could any of the other
panelists, I can pick on you, but

00:40:38.869 --> 00:40:41.600
anyone want to share
their global experience?

00:40:41.600 --> 00:40:42.680
&gt;&gt; I can share mine.

00:40:42.680 --> 00:40:47.953
So I went to Thailand and Cambodia, and
we were focused on human trafficking.

00:40:47.953 --> 00:40:49.496
So it was pretty intense.

00:40:49.496 --> 00:40:54.116
And part of the global study trip we
were actually passing out condoms in

00:40:54.116 --> 00:40:56.511
the red light district in Thailand.

00:40:56.511 --> 00:40:59.075
And I was able to talk to sex workers and

00:40:59.075 --> 00:41:02.604
just really understand all
the societal factors and

00:41:02.604 --> 00:41:08.236
everything that went into these people's
lives, and it was really interesting.

00:41:08.236 --> 00:41:10.128
And I was part of a club at the GSB.

00:41:10.128 --> 00:41:14.011
We were focused on human trafficking and
ways we can help, and

00:41:14.011 --> 00:41:15.512
so super interesting.

00:41:15.512 --> 00:41:21.232
And I think what has stuck with me is
just meeting people who were similarly

00:41:21.232 --> 00:41:27.241
passionate and who cared, and
having these conversations was incredible.

00:41:27.241 --> 00:41:31.348
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks, Daniel, could you
share maybe how things changed for

00:41:31.348 --> 00:41:32.659
global experiences?

00:41:32.659 --> 00:41:37.362
Specifically, travel was impacted
in the past year with COVID, so

00:41:37.362 --> 00:41:42.159
could you share how you fulfilled
a global experience requirement?

00:41:42.159 --> 00:41:45.527
&gt;&gt; I was very lucky because I did
mine in December of my first year.

00:41:45.527 --> 00:41:50.978
So I went to Brazil and
did an entrepreneurship study trip,

00:41:50.978 --> 00:41:53.271
but then I did my summer.

00:41:53.271 --> 00:41:57.968
I was going to go to Brazil to work for a
private K-12 network, and then to Colombia

00:41:57.968 --> 00:42:01.998
to work for a nonprofit charter school,
and had to do both virtually.

00:42:01.998 --> 00:42:06.914
So I did SMIF, Social Management
Innovation Fellowship, I think.

00:42:06.914 --> 00:42:12.438
Basically, it's a stipend that they
gave me, I think $1,300 a week for

00:42:12.438 --> 00:42:17.979
ten weeks, and later they paid me for
me to work with these two organizations.

00:42:17.979 --> 00:42:22.114
I stayed, I was under recovery,
I stayed in Palo Alto and

00:42:22.114 --> 00:42:24.362
worked virtually from there.

00:42:24.362 --> 00:42:31.225
I got a lot of, maybe half of my class
pre-COVID fulfilled their study trip,

00:42:31.225 --> 00:42:35.355
and then the others did
virtual experiences.

00:42:35.355 --> 00:42:40.437
Yeah, I think two, three day virtual
sessions with some of the speakers that

00:42:40.437 --> 00:42:45.454
were going to be in the country, speak in
the country where they were going to go.

00:42:45.454 --> 00:42:51.471
But I would say, hopefully everyone
can all travel in the next year or

00:42:51.471 --> 00:42:53.461
in the next few years.

00:42:53.461 --> 00:42:57.880
But I think the global study trips
are just an incredible experience

00:42:57.880 --> 00:43:01.910
because they're very well run,
not only logistically, but

00:43:01.910 --> 00:43:05.103
the quality of the speakers
is just incredible.

00:43:05.103 --> 00:43:10.295
And the MBA2s lead the study trips,
and they put a lot of work into it,

00:43:10.295 --> 00:43:14.167
and most of the leader
are either from that country or

00:43:14.167 --> 00:43:16.999
are very interested in that country.

00:43:16.999 --> 00:43:21.551
And then the ideas we have and
just facilitates a lot of the alumni

00:43:21.551 --> 00:43:26.524
networks to get the best speakers,
the best of the best speakers, and

00:43:26.524 --> 00:43:30.419
experiences in those trips,
and you have a lot of fun.

00:43:30.419 --> 00:43:34.117
There's some partying involved and
everything,

00:43:34.117 --> 00:43:39.111
so it's just a great combination of,
these are 30 student trips.

00:43:39.111 --> 00:43:45.743
So you create a great bond with
the people that go to the trips.

00:43:45.743 --> 00:43:46.664
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks for sharing.

00:43:46.664 --> 00:43:50.125
Claire, could you share how you fulfilled
your global experience requirement?

00:43:50.125 --> 00:43:55.749
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I went to Singapore in Indonesia,
and our trip was focused

00:43:55.749 --> 00:44:01.486
on ride sharing and ride hailing and
the growth of the gig economy.

00:44:01.486 --> 00:44:05.446
And so fairly different from
different sort of maybe

00:44:05.446 --> 00:44:08.236
education-focused initiatives.

00:44:08.236 --> 00:44:12.362
And actually in reflecting on different
classes that I found most valuable at

00:44:12.362 --> 00:44:16.806
school, some of them were just completely
different from anything that I had known

00:44:16.806 --> 00:44:17.580
previously.

00:44:17.580 --> 00:44:24.263
And I think actually exposure to
stuff in the for profit sector and

00:44:24.263 --> 00:44:30.594
in different pockets just really
helped me push my thinking.

00:44:30.594 --> 00:44:33.749
And kind of one of the reasons I
went to school in the first place is

00:44:33.749 --> 00:44:35.688
because I didn't know what I didn't know.

00:44:35.688 --> 00:44:41.582
And so I actually think looking back,
it's sort of crazy the sort of people and

00:44:41.582 --> 00:44:48.120
things that you're exposed to, people in
different places of power and government.

00:44:48.120 --> 00:44:53.356
Talked a ton about how politics influences
different business decisions in Indonesia,

00:44:53.356 --> 00:44:56.787
where there are different sort
of pockets of corruption.

00:44:56.787 --> 00:44:59.041
And just sort of thinking about,

00:44:59.041 --> 00:45:04.434
I was also thinking about the politics
of education reform, and just different

00:45:04.434 --> 00:45:09.684
ways that they had approached challenges
felt like a useful kind of new lens.

00:45:09.684 --> 00:45:11.045
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks for sharing.

00:45:11.045 --> 00:45:14.556
And there is one question, and
I think Daniel, you were involved in this,

00:45:14.556 --> 00:45:16.410
you mentioned the social impact fund.

00:45:16.410 --> 00:45:20.056
If you could just share a little
bit more about what that is and

00:45:20.056 --> 00:45:22.785
how as a student you can
get involved in that.

00:45:22.785 --> 00:45:25.938
Is it a course or
is it more treated like a club?

00:45:25.938 --> 00:45:30.087
If you could just share a little
bit about the social impact then.

00:45:30.087 --> 00:45:33.070
&gt;&gt; Sure, so
it's a club that you have to apply.

00:45:33.070 --> 00:45:37.299
I think last year was, I think, SMIF,

00:45:37.299 --> 00:45:41.657
there's seven groups within the fund,

00:45:41.657 --> 00:45:46.410
education, healthcare, fintech, etc.

00:45:46.410 --> 00:45:50.414
There's seven groups, and
each group has between six and

00:45:50.414 --> 00:45:53.610
seven students,
who are mostly first years.

00:45:53.610 --> 00:45:56.080
Each group is led by an MBA2.

00:45:56.080 --> 00:46:01.820
And basically, I think last year
the acceptance rate was 30% or 20%.

00:46:01.820 --> 00:46:02.845
A lot of people applied.

00:46:02.845 --> 00:46:10.096
It's an incredible investing experience,
the most similar thing to VC.

00:46:10.096 --> 00:46:13.996
So you apply in November,
December of your first year, and

00:46:13.996 --> 00:46:16.648
you have to enter with your team leader.

00:46:16.648 --> 00:46:21.560
Each team leader creates their own group,
and they basically go through

00:46:21.560 --> 00:46:25.682
the entire experience of
sourcing companies in your field.

00:46:25.682 --> 00:46:29.448
So you talk with funds that
invest in education, so

00:46:29.448 --> 00:46:33.399
with all the education VCs for
them to send you deals.

00:46:33.399 --> 00:46:37.337
And basically, you define a theme
within the group, you internally,

00:46:37.337 --> 00:46:41.803
you talk with the founders, and then you
decide which ones you want to invest, and

00:46:41.803 --> 00:46:43.591
then you vote in turn as a group.

00:46:43.591 --> 00:46:48.362
And the top two startups,
these are early stages, are seed and

00:46:48.362 --> 00:46:51.613
series A startups that are raising funds.

00:46:51.613 --> 00:46:53.723
And then the top two
startups with every group,

00:46:53.723 --> 00:46:55.638
you take them to an investment committee.

00:46:55.638 --> 00:47:01.891
An investment committees is composed
by some faculty members and

00:47:01.891 --> 00:47:08.722
just very experienced investors in
the impact investment community.

00:47:08.722 --> 00:47:14.781
And then you pitch those to investment
committee and then everyone votes,

00:47:14.781 --> 00:47:20.272
both the investment committee members and
the other team members.

00:47:20.272 --> 00:47:22.285
So the good thing is that you
go through the entire process.

00:47:22.285 --> 00:47:27.728
Even if your deal or the company
you're pitching, if they don't pass,

00:47:27.728 --> 00:47:32.995
you see the entire thing, the entire
process of everyone pitching and

00:47:32.995 --> 00:47:38.799
the back and forth of questions between
the investment committee members and

00:47:38.799 --> 00:47:40.784
the people from the fund.

00:47:40.784 --> 00:47:47.021
And at the end, the fund chooses between
two and four startups every year,

00:47:47.021 --> 00:47:51.883
and they get a $50,000 investment,
more or less.

00:47:51.883 --> 00:47:54.786
And actually, a lot of people do that, and

00:47:54.786 --> 00:47:59.500
then they have the experience to
then get a job in impact investing.

00:47:59.500 --> 00:48:00.870
Or in venture capital.

00:48:00.870 --> 00:48:04.618
So I think it's the best way for those
that want to get into an investing role,

00:48:04.618 --> 00:48:06.590
is just the most incredible way to do it.

00:48:06.590 --> 00:48:08.960
And then get a summer job at a farm.

00:48:08.960 --> 00:48:12.390
And then hopefully you get
a full-time offer, those GSB.

00:48:12.390 --> 00:48:13.760
